{
  "correction_rules": [
    "General bias: model is systematically too low in the first ~1–20 forecast days during rising regimes (typical underprediction ~+0.3 to +2.0 EUR, occasionally more). Apply an upward level shift when the last 5–10 days show a positive slope.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: beyond ~day 20 the model often becomes too bearish/volatile and can drift away from the prevailing level (frequently forecasting a drop that does not materialize). Damp long-horizon moves by shrinking deviations from the recent mean (e.g., pull days 20–30 30–50% toward the last-10-day average).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: when the model projects sharp upside spikes (e.g., >+2 EUR within a few days), realized prices tend to rise but less than forecast. Cap near-term step changes; scale forecasted daily jumps by ~0.6–0.8 when consecutive up-days are predicted.",
    "Mean-reversion mis-timing: the model tends to call mean reversion too early after an uptrend (predicting flattening/decline while the market stays elevated). Delay mean reversion signals by ~3–7 days when momentum is positive (last 5-day return > 0).",
    "Amplitude compression around the plateau: once prices reach a higher plateau (~68–70 in these samples), the model’s oscillations are too large (both up and down). Reduce forecast variance around the plateau by smoothing (e.g., 3–5 day moving average on the forecast path) rather than changing the level."
  ]
}
